      "transcript_text": "Let us delve into our first topic which is Principles of Finance. So here we will talk about two important principles of Finance. I will not reveal what those principles are. Believe it or not, all of you already know what those principles are. These principles are already there at the back of your minds. What I am essentially going to do is I am going to pose you a few open questions and after answering those questions, you will be able to know what those principles are. Okay. So let us move to our principle one. Okay. Suppose you were given with two choices. The question before you is which of the two choices would you prefer? Here are the choices. Choice 1 entails receiving one lakh rupees today with 100% probability which means that choice 1 does not entail any risk. Okay. Let us move to choice 2. Choice 2 entails receiving exactly one lakh rupees. after a year. Again with 100% probability which means that there is no risk in the case of choice 2 as well. So therefore we have choice 1 which entails receiving one lakh rupees today for sure and choice 2 which entails receiving one lakh rupees exactly after a year for sure. Which of the two choices would you prefer? Take a pause and think about the same thing. I am sure all of you almost all of you would prefer choice 1. Did you realize as to why you choose choice 1 over choice 2? The answer is simple. If you receive one lakh rupees today, you can invest this sum of one lakh rupees in your bank 60 positive. 60 positive let us say and get more than one lakh rupees exactly after a year. And if you are a rational person which I assume you are, you will prefer more than one lakh over just one lakh. So what does this principle tell us? This principle tells us that a rupee that you receive today is worth more than a rupee received in the future. In other words, this principle tells us that money has time value. So this principle as I told you was already there at the back of your minds. I just made you recall that principle.",
