      "transcript_text": "Let us move on to our next topic, which is about evaluating investment decisions. Suppose you are given that the corporation has an investment project and it also has sufficient cash to fund the project. You understand that the financial goal of the corporation is to maximize the shareholder wealth, which essentially amounts to maximizing the current market value of shares. Now there is a decision dilemma before the financial manager. Financial manager has to make a choice, has to make a decision. Should the financial manager invest cash in the project or should she or he pay tout as dividend to shareholders? This is the decision dilemma before the financial manager. Let us put this information pictorially. Let us look at this figure. So you have an investment project. And you have two choices as the financial manager of the firm. Either you can invest this cash in this project. Alternatively, you can pay out this cash as dividend to your shareholders. In which case, your shareholders can invest for themselves in the financial market on their own. Let me give you a question. Give you some additional information. The additional information which is given to you is that this project carries a risk of beta. Beta is a hypothetical number. And this project is going to give you a return of let us say R1. On the other hand, if the financial manager decides to pay out cash as dividend to shareholders, and if shareholders invest in those assets, which carry a risk of beta, in that case they are going to get a return of R2. Under what condition should the financial manager decide to invest cash in the project? Let me reiterate. The answer is as long as R1 is greater than R2, it makes sense for the manager to invest cash in the project. This brings us to a very important concept of opportunity cost. You must have come across this concept in your day to day lives. What is opportunity cost? Opportunity cost is the benefit that you as an individual forgo, when you choose one alternative over another, at the same time. What is the opportunity cost of taking up the project? Is it R1 or is it R2? What do you think? Or is it something else? Think about the definition of opportunity cost. It is the benefit that you forgo, when you choose one alternative over another, at the same level of risk.",
