      "transcript_text": "[music] So let us talk about a bond and its features. What is a bond? A bond is essentially a financial asset. Who issues a bond? A bond can be issued by a corporation. A bond can also be issued by a government. Why do corporations and governments issue bonds? Corporations and governments issue bonds so as to raise money. Whom is a bond issued to? A bond is issued to investors like you and me. What do investors get for purchasing a bond? investors are essentially promised some payments which they are entitled to get in the future. Yet another question you might have is how long do these payments last? These payments are going to last until the date of maturity of the bond. Yet another question you might have is what are the different forms that these payments take? These payments typically take two forms. One is the payment of interest period after period and this interest is what we call as coupon in the terminology of a bond. And finally at the time of maturity of the bond you get another payment which is the payment of face value. Face value is nothing but it is a notional value which is given in a bond certificate and this value does not change irrespective of whatever happens. Let us now look at the life cycle of a bond. What happens when you buy a bond? So when you buy a bond, the issuer gives you the bond and you give to the issuer money. Okay, fair enough. What happens during the time you hold the bond? So during the time you hold the bond, you will be receiving interest payments period after period. And as I told you previously, these periodic interest payments are called as coupon payments. Finally, what happens at the time of maturity of the bond? At the time of maturity of the bond, you will receive your last coupon payment. And along with this coupon payment, you will also receive something called as face value. Now this face value is also known as par value and face value or par value never changes as I told you irrespective of market conditions. You might be having this question as to how we determine our coupon payment. Now coupon payments are determined using coupon rate and again this coupon rate is specified in a bond certificate and this coupon rate also never changes irrespective of change in market conditions. Now this coupon rate has two important features. The first feature is that this coupon rate is always expressed as a percentage of the face value. And the second feature of coupon rate is that this rate is expressed as an APR as an annual percentage rate. And therefore, if you have to find out coupon rate every period, what will you do? You will divide this APR by M where M is the number of coupon payments in a year. Let us look at one example. Suppose there is $100 face value bond with a 10% coupon rate and annual payments. So therefore, how much coupon are you going to get every year? So first of all let us look at coupon rate every year. Coupon rate every year will be APR by M. It is given to you as 10% and here M is one because the bond is making one payment in a year. Yes. So therefore your coupon rate every year is 10%. And this is 10% of what? This is 10% of the face value which is dollar 100 in this case. So 10% of dollar 100 comes out to be dollar10. So therefore you will be getting a coupon of $110 every year from this bond. Let us look at another example. Suppose we have a $100 face value bond with a 10% coupon rate and semianual coupon payments. So in this case how much coupon will you get every half year? Again let us first of all try to find out coupon rate every half year. So to find that out we will divide our APR by M. APR is given to us as 10%. And here our M is going to be two because there are two half years in a year. So therefore coupon rate every half year is 5%. And this 5% is of what? This is 5% of the face value which is again dollar 100. So therefore you are going to get 5% of $100 which is $15 every 6 months as your coupon payment from this bond. Now let me talk about a bond certificate. Now a bond certificate specifies several things. It specifies face value. It specifies coupon rate. It also specifies how frequently the coupons are going to be paid and it also specifies the date of maturity among other things. None of these things change irrespective of what happens in the market. Your face value does not change. Your coupon rate does not change. Your frequency of coupon payment does not change and your date of maturity also does not change. >> [music]",
