      "transcript_text": "[music] >> Now let us talk about as to how to value a bond with given characteristics. Suppose face value of the bond is F. And as you understand, this is going to be paid to you at the time of maturity of the bond. And let us say that the bond is going to make you a payment of coupon of C rupees every period. And how do we find this coupon of C rupees every period? We multiply our coupon rate every period with face value. And let us say that there are T periods to maturity. Okay. Let us try to put our cash flows on a timeline from this bond. So we have a bond. Period after period, the bond is going to give you a payment of C rupees. And this C is nothing but coupon payment or interest payment. And the bond is going to mature in T periods from today. And therefore at the time of maturity you will not only get your last coupon payment of C rupees, but you will also get your face value F. Yes? So we understand that present value of a bond is equal to present value of coupon payments plus present value of face value. And as we discussed, since these coupons are going to be made to you period after period, so therefore these coupons take the form of an annuity. And the payment of face value, as you can make out, it is only one-time payment. Okay? So in this case, do we know how to find out present value of an annuity? The answer is yes. What is present value of an annuity which pays you C rupees every period for T periods? That is simply going to be C by R into 1 minus 1 divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T. What is going to be the present value of face value F that you are going to receive after T periods from today? It will simply be F divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T. And as we discussed, if the market is functioning fairly, then the present value of the bond has to be equal to the price of the bond. So the price, as you know, it is the amount you have to pay or you will get if you buy or sell this bond. So therefore, price of the bond is equal to essentially C by R into 1 minus 1 divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T + F divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T. Now, the question is what is this R called as? Recall from our previous discussion, this R is nothing but opportunity cost for bond holders. Yes? And obviously, this depends on the risk of the bond. The more risky a bond is, the greater is going to be its opportunity cost. In terminology of a bond, we call this R as yield to maturity or simply yield. You must be wondering, why do we call this R or opportunity cost as yield to maturity? We call it as yield to maturity because this is the return that bond holders expect from buying this bond today and holding it until its maturity. And obviously, the more risky a bond, the greater is going to be the return that investors will expect from this bond. Now, there is another thing which is worth noting, which is that yield to maturity is also expressed as an APR. Let me repeat. Similar to coupon rate, even your yield to maturity is expressed as an APR. And therefore, if you were to find out yield every period, what will you do? You will divide the given APR by M, where M is the number of compounding intervals in a year. of compounding intervals [music] in a year.",
