      "transcript_text": "[music] >> Now let us talk about another classification of bonds based on the type of payments that they promise us. So far we have seen that bonds typically make us two kinds of payments. One is the payment of coupon period after period and the second one is the payment of face value which occurs at the time of maturity. And these traditional bonds are also called as coupon bonds. So therefore our coupon bonds make us two kinds of payments. One is the payment of coupon period after period and other one is the payment of face value. So we call these bonds as coupon bonds. However, remember that coupon bonds are not the only kind of bonds. We also have something called as zero coupon bonds. Zero coupon bonds, as the name implies they do not carry any coupon. Which means that if you hold a zero coupon bond you will not be getting any coupon payment. The only payment which you will be getting is going to be the payment of face value at the time of maturity of the bond. Now my question to you is zero coupon bonds are these bonds always going to be discount bonds or are they going to be par bonds or are they going to be premium bonds? What do you think? Assume that we will never have negative interest rate environment. In other words, you can assume that our R is always going to be a positive number. Under this assumption, our zero coupon bonds, will they be discount bonds, par bonds, or premium bonds? What do you think? So, we understand that the price of a bond equals C by R into 1 minus 1 divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T plus F divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T. In the case of a zero coupon bond, since you are going to get a zero coupon, therefore, your first term will vanish. And therefore, your price will simply be equal to F divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T. And as we discussed previously, in this course, we will assume that our R is always going to be a positive number. And if that is the case, then this term is always going to be less than F. So, what happens when the price of a bond is less than its face value? We call such a bond as a discount bond. And therefore, zero coupon bonds are also called as pure discount bonds because they always trade at a discount. For example, if we talk about government securities which mature in less than a year, they are typically zero coupon securities. Now, you must be wondering if these are zero coupon bonds, why do we buy these bonds? You buy or sell a bond at its price and not at its face value. Face value is a notional value which is given in a bond certificate and it never changes. So, therefore, when you buy a zero coupon bond, and let us say that the face value of the bond is $1,000, you will always be purchasing these zero coupon bonds at a discount. Which means that you will be paying less than $1,000 for purchasing the bonds today. And at the time of maturity, you will be getting a face value of $1,000. Let us talk about another kind of bonds which are called as perpetual bonds. Perpetual bonds, as the name implies, these bonds are perpetual. Which means that these bonds do not have a date of maturity. If these bonds do not have a date of maturity, it means that you are never going to get your face value ever. And therefore, in the case of a perpetual bond, you will only get coupon payments period after period, but you will never get your face value ever. So, if we talk about additional tier one bonds, which are issued by commercial banks in India, these bonds are typically perpetual bonds. These bonds, in short, are called as AT1 bonds, additional tier one bonds. However, these bonds are callable bonds, which means that the commercial bank which has issued these AT1 bonds has an option to call these bonds back if required. However, that is not an obligation. So, therefore, if the commercial banks never decide to call these bonds back, these will be perpetual bonds.",
