      "transcript_text": "[music] >> Now let us talk about government bonds or treasury securities. Depending on the maturity of government or treasury securities, we can classify them into three broad kinds. First of all, we have treasury bills. Treasury bills mature in a year or less. We also have something called as treasury notes, which mature in 1 to 10 years. And finally, we have something called as treasury bonds, which mature in more than 10 years. Yes? So, we have T-bills or treasury bills maturing in less than a year. We have treasury notes or T-notes maturing in 1 to 10 years. And we have treasury bonds or T-bonds maturing in more than 10 years. Now, as we discussed, the face value of treasury securities or government bonds varies from one country to another country. If we talk about US government bonds, they have a face value of $1,000. If we talk about Indian government bonds, they have a face value of 100 rupees. Similarly, if we talk about frequency of interest payments or coupon payments, that also varies from country to country. So, if we talk about Indian government bonds or US government bonds or bonds issued by UK and Canada and Japan governments. So, they pay interest semi-annually. On the other hand, if we talk about Eurobonds, most of them pay coupons or interest annually. Now, let us talk about identifying government bonds. How do we name or identify government bonds? Government bonds are typically identified uniquely by a combination of their coupon rate and year of maturity. While in theory, it is possible to have two government bonds to carry the same coupon and maturing in the same year, governments do not typically issue bonds that mature in the same year and also carry the same coupon rate. So, therefore, we can uniquely identify a government bond using a combination of its coupon rate and year of maturity. For example, if we have a government bond that is carrying a coupon rate of 7.1% and which is maturing in 2030, in the US context, we will call this bond as 7.1s of 2030. In the Indian setting, we will call these bonds as 7.1 GS 2030. What does GS stand for? GS stands for government security. Now, let us talk about quotes of government bonds. We will focus our attention on US government bonds or US Treasury securities. So we can look at the quotes of these bonds on Wall Street Journal. So when you want to buy or sell a government bond, obviously you will have to look at its quote. For example, if you want to purchase foreign currency or forex, you go to a forex dealer. And the forex dealer typically quotes you two kinds of prices. One is the bid price and other one is the ask price. Okay. Similarly, when you want to buy or sell a bond, typically you have to deal with an intermediary called as a bond dealer. Now similar to a forex dealer, a bond dealer will also quote you two kinds of prices. One is the bid price and the second one is the ask price. Okay. What is bid price? Bid price is the price at which a bond dealer is willing to buy a given bond. What is ask price? Similarly, ask price is the price at which a bond dealer is willing to sell a given bond. Now suppose you want to buy a bond. So therefore, which price is relevant for you? Is it the bid price or is it the ask price? For you, ask price is relevant. Why? Because when you want to buy a bond from a bond dealer, you will only be able to buy if the bond dealer is willing to sell you that bond. And you understand that the bond dealer is willing to sell the bond at the ask price. So therefore, when you want to buy a bond, for you, the ask price is relevant. In a similar way, when you want to sell a bond, which price is relevant? Is it the bid price or is it the ask price? So when you want to sell a bond, for you, the ask price is not relevant. For you, the bid price is relevant. Why? Because you will be able to sell a bond to the bond dealer only if the bond dealer is willing to buy that bond. And the bond dealer is willing to buy that bond at the bid price. So therefore, in nutshell, when you want to buy a bond, for you, ask price is relevant. When you want to sell a bond, for you, bid price is relevant. Now let us look at quote of this one bond. So this bond is maturing on 15th August 2028. This bond is carrying a coupon rate of 5.5%. And this coupon rate, as you understand, this is expressed as a percentage of face value, and this is expressed as an APR. Now let us talk about the bid price. Again, both bid and ask prices are expressed as a percentage of face value. For example, in this case, the bid price has been shown as 105.01. It means that it is 105.01% of the face value. And since we are talking about US government bonds, you understand that the face value of US government bonds is always $1,000. So, therefore, the bid price here in dollars is 1050.1 dollars. And similarly, ask price also has been expressed as a percentage of the face value. And therefore, your ask price in dollars is 1050.2 dollars. Okay. What do you notice here? You notice that your ask price is higher than your bid price. Why? The reason is that the bond dealer is willing to sell you the same bond at a higher price than he or she is willing to buy that bond at. And this difference between the ask price and bid price represents the profit that a bond dealer makes. And this difference between the ask price and the bid price is what we call as the bid-ask spread. And bid-ask spread, therefore, represents how a bond dealer makes money. Finally, we have something called as ask yield. What is ask yield? Ask yield is yield to maturity based on the ask price. And this is given to us as 3.694% Again, this is expressed as a percentage. Yes? And this is expressed as an APR. You should always remember that. And here your coupon rate, bid price, and ask price, these have been expressed as percentages of face value. And face value of US government bonds, as we know, are always $1,000. >> [music]",
