      "transcript_text": "Now let us try to solve some problems on bonds. Let us first of all, look at problem 1. A 15-year bond is issued with a face value of Rs.0000 paying interest of Rs.50 per year. If interest rates increase shortly after the bond is issued, the question before you is what happens to the bonds coupon rate? Coop on rate as you can understand is not going to change irrespective of changes in market conditions. So therefore your coupon rate does not change. What happens to part B, your face value the face value of the bond again does not change because it remains constant irrespective of changes in market conditions. Coop on rate as well as face value they are specified in a bond certificate and none of them change once a bond is issued. Let us look at part C. What happens to the bonds yield to maturity? If your interest rates in the economy have increased, what will happen to yield to maturity on all bonds in general? The yields to maturity on all bonds in general will also go up with increase in interest rates. So therefore your yield to maturity goes up. And finally let us look at part D. What happens to the bonds price? Because yield to maturity on the bond has gone up. Therefore price is going to go down. Why? Because prices of bonds are inversely proportional to their yield to maturity. Now let us look at another problem. Problem 2. In December 2020 US Treasury 4 and 1-4 of 2040 offered semi annually compounded yield to maturity of 1.32 percent. Recognizing that coupons are paid semi annually calculate the bonds price. So we have talked about similar kinds of examples before you are given with features of the bond and what you have to do is you have to find out the bonds price. Let us try to find it out. We understand that since this is a US government bond, therefore the face value of the bond will be thousand dollars. Can we find out coupon payments? Again since this is a US government bond, this is going to make coupon payments semi annually. Do we have information on coupon rate given to us? If we look at the problem, it says the coupon rate is 4 and 1-4 which means 4.25 percent. So therefore your coupon rate every half year is 4.25 percent divided by 2 and this percentage is of what? This percentage is of face value which is dollar thousand. And therefore how much coupon will you get every half year? You will get therefore a coupon of 21.25 dollars every half year. Do we have information on R? The yield to maturity? The yield to maturity is given to us as 1.32 percent. Again since this is expressed as an APR, we will divide it by 2 to arrive at yield every half year. So we arrive at an yield of 0.66 percent every half year. Do we have information on number of half years to maturity? It is December 2020 today and the bond is going to mature. In 2040, so therefore how many years do we have to maturity? We have 20 years and therefore 40 half years. So we have 40 half years to maturity and we can find out the price of the bond. How? By using bond pricing equation? Which says that price equals C by R into 1 minus 1 divided by 1 plus R raised to the power T plus F divided by 1 plus R raised to the power T. C is given to us as 21.25 R is given to us as 0.0066 into 1 minus 1 divided by 1.0066 raised to the power 40 plus 1000 divided by 1. divided by 1.0066 raised to the power 40. What does that give you? That gives you dollar 1513.55. So this is going to be the price of the bond. However, let me ask you one question. Even before solving this problem, could you have figured out whether the price should be more than 1000 or should it be less than 1000? Could you have figured it out even before you solve the problem? The answer is yes. Why? Because you know what is the coupon rate? The coupon rate is 4.25 percent. The yield to maturity is 1.32 percent. And clearly, since the coupon rate is greater than yield to maturity, it tells you that the bond is currently trading at a premium. And for a premium bond, we understand that price is greater than face value. And therefore, our price should have been greater than 1000 dollars in the first place. So therefore, if your answer were to be less than 1000 dollars, it would have told you that you have clearly made an error. Because the price of a bond which is trading at a premium cannot be less than 1000 dollars.",
