      "transcript_text": "Now, when you want to buy or sell a share, obviously you will have to place an order. So, these orders at a broad level, we can classify them into two types. So, orders can be broadly classified into two kinds. We have market orders and we also have something called as limit orders. First of all, let us talk about a market order. A market order is that order which is executed immediately. It is executed immediately. So, essentially, when you place a market order, you specify two things. You specify the ticker that you want to buy or sell and you specify the number of shares that you want to buy or sell. Obviously, any order could be either an order to buy shares or it can be an order to sell shares. So, therefore, our market order can be either market or it can be market sell order. So, now, what is a market buy order and what is a market sell order. Now, just like when you want to trade in bonds, you encounter two kinds of prices, bid prices and ask prices. Similarly, when you want to buy or sell shares, you encounter two kinds of prices, bid prices and ask prices. What is a bid price? Bid prices that price at which other investors are willing to buy a given share. What is ask price? Similarly, ask prices that price at which investors are willing to sell a given share. Now, my question to you is suppose you want to buy a share, which price is relevant for you? Is it the bid price or is it the ask price? Obviously, when you want to buy a share for you, ask price is relevant. Why? Because you can only buy a share if somebody else is willing to sell you that share and somebody else is willing to sell you that share at the ask price. In a similar way, when you want to sell a share, which price is relevant for you? Is it the bid price or is it the ask price? It has to be the bid price. When you want to sell a share for you, the bid price is relevant. Why? Because you will be able to sell a share only if somebody is willing to buy that share and investors are willing to buy those shares at the bid price. Now, let us focus our attention on buy orders. When you place an order to buy shares, you understand that for you, the ask price is relevant. Now, different investors will be willing to sell the same share at different prices. And therefore, at what price will you buy those shares? Obviously, you would want to buy shares at a price as low as possible. So, therefore, for you, the lowest ask price is relevant. In other words, if you place a market buy order, it will be executed at the lowest ask price. Similarly, when you place a market sell order, obviously for you, the bid price is relevant. And as you understand, different investors are willing to buy the same share at different prices. And therefore, at what price will you sell your shares? You will sell your shares at as high price as possible. In other words, which price is relevant for you? In other words, highest bid prices relevant for you. So, therefore, when you place a market sell order, it will be executed at the highest bid price. So, market sell orders will be executed at the highest bid price.",
