      "transcript_text": "After having talked about trading in common stocks, we will now talk about valuation of common stocks. Let me begin this topic by asking you a question. My question is, can you use the book value of a firm as a measure of its money? market value? What problems do you think you will encounter with this approach? When I am saying book value, essentially I am asking you to refer to values which are given in the balance sheet. Can you use values in the balance sheet of a firm as a measure of market values of those values? Assets, in other words, the answer is no. But why? Because if you look at a balance sheet, balance sheet essentially captures the asset values at historical cost, which means it captures the cost at the time of buying the asset. Over a period of time, these costs will become historical and they may be not represent the true market value of the asset today. Also, book values usually do not include intangible assets. For example, your brand name and therefore, book values may not give you a measure of market values. And finally, book values also do not capture going concern value. What is going concern value? Going concern value is that value which is the value for business as a going concern, which means that when the business is operating as a healthy operating unit. In that case, what is the value of the business in the market? Now, this going concern value is created when a collection of assets is organized into a healthy operating business. For example, it is possible that there are synergies between your assets and therefore, the market value of your business is more than the sum of the values of individual assets. So, because of these reasons, book value cannot be taken as a measure of the market value. If we cannot take book value as a measure of market value, obviously, we need to look at techniques as to how to find out value of a firm or a firm's stock. So, here, we will limit our discussion to valuation of stock of a firm. And we are going to talk about two approaches to valuing the stock price of a firm. The first approach is going to be valuation by comparables. This approach is also called as comparable companies analysis or relative valuation. And the second approach we will look at is called as valuation by fundamentals. It is the same valuation which we arrive at using discounted cash flow approach. And therefore, we also call it as discounted cash flow valuation. This approach is also called as dividend discount model.",
