      "transcript_text": "Let us talk about the first approach to value the shares of a firm. This approach is called as Stock Valuation by Comparables. This approach is also called as Comparable Companies Analysis because we make use of Comparable Companies to find out the stock value. of price of our firm and this approach is also called as Relative Valuation. Why? Because our valuation is relative to the valuation of Comparable Companies. Let us talk about the steps involved in this approach. The first step involved in this approach is to identify Comparables. Now, the question is what are Comparables? A Comparable is a firm which is comparable to the firm whose stock price you want to find out. Now, the question is what is the meaning of a Comparable? How do we identify a Comparable? To identify a Comparable, we look at those firms which are in the stock value. Same industry as the industry of the firm whose stock price we want to find out. So, essentially Comparables mean those firms which are in the same industry. So, we essentially look at firms in the same industry as our Comparables. Now, the second step involved in this approach is to choose a suitable multiple. Choose a suitable multiple or a ratio. For example, we could choose price to earnings ratio as our multiple. We could choose price to book ratio as our multiple or we could choose some other multiple for valuation. Now, in the example that we are going to talk about in a short while, we will focus on price to earnings multiple for valuation. So, therefore, let me ask you what is the meaning of price to earnings ratio or price to earnings multiple? Simply, if you look at this ratio, this is price per share to earnings per share. So, what does that tell you? Intuitively, intuitively it tells you that how much are investors willing to pay for one rupee of earnings of a firm. Now, you understand that different firms trade at different PE multiples. So, therefore, my question to you is why do you think investors are willing to pay different amount for one rupee of earnings of different firms? Let me repeat, why are investors willing to pay different price for one rupee of earnings of different firms? The answer is simple because it is possible that future growth in earnings of one firm could be higher than that of the other firm. And therefore, investors are willing to pay higher price for one rupee of earnings of that firm whose growth in earnings in the future is going to be higher. Now, let us talk about our third step of the approach. So, as a third step, you find out mean or median value of the chosen multiple for your comparables. So, you find out the mean or median value of the chosen multiple for our comparables. So, since we are choosing price to earnings ratio, we will find out the mean value or the median value of the P ratio for our comparables. Now, the assumption here is that our firm whose stock price we want to find out that is going to trade at similar multiple or ratio as that of our comparables. And under this assumption, we should be able to find out the stock price of our firm. So, therefore, as a last step, the stock price of our firm, is simply going to be mean price to earnings ratio of comparables multiplied by earnings of the firm. Now, literally if you see here, this E will get cancelled out and you will arrive at the stock price of your firm.",
