      "transcript_text": "Having said that, now let me ask you, what do you think are challenges in using this approach? So there are a couple of challenges in using this approach. The first challenge lies in our first step. Our first step as you recall was to identify comparables. Now you understand that no two firms in this world are alike. So which means that you cannot find a perfect comparable for a firm. In other words, you do not know how comparable is your comparable. Okay. Now let me ask you another question. Can I use price to earnings ratio as my multiple? For finding out the stock price of a company using valuation by comparable's approach is price to earnings ratio as a multiple suitable for valuing all kinds of firms. The answer is not necessarily. price to earnings ratio may not be a very suitable multiple for specially startups. You often observe that startups in their initial few years make losses. In other words, their earnings are often negative. And therefore, if you try to find out the stock price by multiplying mean, price to earnings ratio of your comparables with negative earnings, what will you get? You will get negative estimate of stock price, which is absurd. So essentially, nobody is going to give you the stock and at the same time going to pay you the money as well. Okay. So which means that price to earnings ratio is not suitable for valuing all kinds of companies. In other words, we cannot make use of all multiples to value all kinds of firms. Some multiples are suitable for certain kinds of firms and some others are suitable for other kinds of firms. Okay. Now one last question again, which is a challenge to using this approach. If you look at HUL, HUL, is from households and personal products industry. And here I have taken four comparables for HUL. I have taken Godridge consumer, Dabber India, Colgate and Mariko as comparables for HUL. Now my question to you is, can you identify another challenge of valuation by comparable approach by looking at comparable data for HUL's comparables? Okay. If you look here, you observe that the P ratio range. For comparables of HUL, it varies from 42 to 62. While Dabber India has a P of 42, Godridge consumer has a P ratio of 62. So this range is quite wide. You do not know where in this range, the P ratio of HUL lies. In other words, these ratios are varying widely within the same industry. And therefore, in this case, we may not have too much of confidence about our estimate of stock price. So these are few of the challenges which we encounter while using valuation by comparable approach.",
