      "transcript_text": "Now we will talk about the second method to value stocks. This method is called as stock valuation by fundamentals or basics. This is also known as Discung Treat Cash Flow Valuation. So let us delve into this method of finding out the stock price or stock value. How do we find out the present value of any asset? The present value of any asset as you know, it is present value of all expected future cash flows from that asset. Now in this course, we will assume that the market is fairly pricing the assets. And if the market is fairly pricing the assets, then the current asset price should be equal to present value of the asset. So therefore, in case the market is fairly pricing the assets, our current asset price has to equal the present value. If the market is over pricing the asset, then your current asset price will be more than the present value of expected future cash flows from the asset. And finally, if the market is under pricing the asset, in that case the current asset price will be less than the present value of expected future cash flows from the asset. In this case, the asset we are interested in finding the value of is stock. Now if you hold a stock or a share, what kind of cash flows are you entitled or promised to receive from this asset? So, in that case if you hold a stock, during your holding period you may receive cash dividends that the company may decide to pay out during your holding period. And also at the end of your holding period, you might sell off the stock in which case you will get the selling price at that particular point in time. So, therefore, what should our current stock price be? Our current stock price as we discussed previously, if the market is fairly pricing the stock, in that case it has to equal present value of the stock. And how do we find out the present value of the stock? We look at future cash flows from the stock and then we discount them to find out their present values. It means that the present value of a stock equals present value of the dividend payments that you are going to receive during the holding period and present value of the selling price that you are going to get at the end of your holding period. Now, let us say that if you hold a stock for one year, if your holding period is one year, in that case what are the cash flows you are going to receive? Let us try to put them on a timeline. So, essentially you are buying the stock today at a price of P0 and at the end of one year from today you may get a dividend let us say die 1 and after which the stock is expected to sell at a price of P1. So, from here when your holding period, when holding period is one year. In that case what is going to be your stock price? It will be present value of the discounted cash flows. The current stock price in this case will be present value of your expected cute future cash flows from holding this stock which means that it will be die 1 divided by 1 plus r plus P1 divided by 1 plus r. Now, the question here is what is r here? You know r is the discount rate. r is shareholders opportunity cost of capital and what does that depend on? That obviously depends on the risk of the stock. The greater the risk of the stock, the higher is going to be shareholders opportunity cost of capital. In other words we can say that your r is the return that shareholders require from this stock. Yes, in the terminology of a stock, this r is also called as the cost of equity. Remember that in the case of a bond we call this r as the yield to maturity. In the case of a stock we call this r as the cost of equity. Now, your holding period need not be one year. Your holding period can well be 2 years. So let us say that if your holding period is 2 years then what are the cash flows that you may receive from this stock? Let us put them on a timeline. So let us say that you are going to receive a dividend of die 1 at the end of one year. A dividend of die 2 at the end of the second year and thereafter the stock is expected to sell at a price of P2. So therefore when your holding period is 2 years, what is your stock price going to be? It is simply going to be die 1 divided by 1 plus r plus die 2 divided by 1 plus r whole square plus P2 divided by 1 plus r whole square. Now remember that your holding period need not even be 2 years. Your holding period could be h years. So if your holding period is let us say h years and let us say that if you are going to receive a dividend of die 1 at the end of one year, die 2 at the end of 2 years from today, die of h at the end of h years from today. And immediately after paying this dividend of die which the stock is expected to sell at a price of pH. So in that case, in that case what will our P0 be? In that case our P0 will be die 1 divided by 1 plus r plus die 2 divided by 1 plus r whole square. And so on and so forth till we have die of h divided by 1 plus r whole raised to the power h plus pH divided by 1 plus r whole raised to the power h. So this implies that my P0 is summation die of t. So this implies my P0 is summation die of t divided by 1 plus r whole raised to the power t where t varies from 1 to h plus pH divided by 1 plus r whole raised to the power h. In this case what is pH called as? In this case pH is called as the terminal or horizon value. In other words we can say that our current stock price is equal to present value of expected dividends until h years plus present value of your terminal value.",
