      "transcript_text": "Until this point in time, we know that your current stock price is equal to present value of dividends expected until H years plus present value of the terminal value. Let us put it on a timeline. So, therefore, you understand that if your holding period is H years and P naught is your current stock price and if you are going to receive a dividend of DY1 at the end of year 1, DY2 at the end of year 2 and DYH at the end of year H and after which the stock is expected to sell at a price of pH, then we can say that our current stock price P naught will be equal to summation DY2 divided by 1 plus R whole raised to the power T where T varies from 1 to H plus pH, DY2 divided by 1 plus R whole raised to the power H. Now, your horizon period H need not be a finite period, H can well be infinite. Why? Because you understand that corporations have perpetual life, unless they encounter events such as bankruptcy or an acquisition and therefore, corporations are also called as going concern. They will keep going on and on until they encounter a bankruptcy or an acquisition. Now, the question is what will happen to the present value formula when H approaches infinity. Just look at our formula and see what will happen when H approaches infinity. As you can see when H approaches infinity, what will happen to this denominator? This denominator will become infinite. In other words, this term will essentially vanish and therefore, we can say that our current stock price when H is infinity, it will be equal to only summation DY2 divided by 1 plus R whole raised to the power T where T varies from 1 to infinity. In other words, we can say that our current stock price is equal to present value of expected future dividends from this stock. If you take into account all future dividends from this stock and discount them and add them up, you will arrive at your present value. And therefore, this particular valuation formula is also called as dividend discount model or DDM insured. Why is it called as dividend discount model? Because this particular formula tells us, that present value or current stock price is equal to present value of all expected future dividends from this stock. Now, our stocks can be of various kinds. So, we could have for example, a zero growth stock, a zero growth stock is that stock whose dividends do not grow, whose growth rate in dividends is zero as the name implies. In other words, if you keep on getting the same dividend period after period, this particular stock will be called as a zero growth stock. We can also have a constant growth stock. What is a constant growth stock? Constant growth stock as the name implies, the dividends from this stock grow at a constant rate. So, let us say that if you are going to get a dividend of DY1 at the end of the first year from today and if your dividends are going to grow at a constant rate of G per year, then your dividend at the end of the second year will be DY1 into 1 plus G. As you can notice, here our dividends are growing at a constant rate G and for us to be able to find out the stock price, we can apply the present value formula for a growing per The only condition is that your G has to be less than R. In other words, your growth rating dividends has to be less than your cost of equity for you to be able to find out the current stock price. Let us also talk about something called as a differential growth stock. What is a differential growth stock? A differential growth stock as the name implies has dividends growing at one rate for a certain number of years and thereafter, dividends grow at another rate in perpetuity. Even the case that in the growth phase of a company, the dividends might grow at a constant rate which is much higher than R, but later on once the company matures, in that case the dividends from this company might grow at another rate G2, which could be less than the cost of equity.",
