      "transcript_text": "Hello, learners! Welcome to the module on Capital Budgeting. Capital Budgeting is the process of firm undertakes to evaluate potential investment projects. So, essentially you understand that a company may have several projects at hand, so which of the projects should be accepted? And which ones should be rejected is decided based on Capital Budgeting Techniques. So, as part of our agenda, we will cover a number of Capital Budgeting Techniques. We already know about Net Present Value or NPV. We will be talking about Payback Period, Discounting Payback Period and Internal Rate of Return as a result of the budget. We will also talk about pros and cons of each of these techniques and then we will also talk about as to how to estimate project related cash flows. Now, I must tell you that a technique is going to lead to inferior decisions if it does not take into account time value of money because it is obvious that time has money value and therefore it should be taken into account. Similarly, a technique is going to lead to inferior decisions if it gets affected by tastes and preferences of managers because we know that tastes and preferences of managers have nothing to do with as to whether a technique is value adding or not. Similarly, a technique will lead to inferior decisions if it gets influenced by the choice of the accounting method. Your accounting method predominantly does not affect your cash flows and therefore, your decision to accept or reject a project should not be influenced by the choice of the accounting method. And finally, a technique will lead to inferior decisions if it depends on the profitability of a company's existing business because profitability of a company's existing business has nothing to do with as to whether a project is value adding or not. All in all, we should accept those projects which increase the wealth of shareholders and we should reject those projects which decrease the wealth of shareholders. Why? Because the financial goal of the corporation is to maximize the shareholder wealth. Now, let us look at this particular figure. What does this figure show you? This figure essentially gives you a snapshot of different techniques which are followed by companies. Now, chief financial officers or CFOs of US companies were asked as to which all techniques do they make use of for evaluating investment projects and this is how they responded. They said 20% of the time they use book rate of return, 57% of the time they make use of payback, 76% of the time they make use of internal rate of return or IRR and NPV is closely followed at 75% of the times. Now, since book rate of return is least used among all the methods so therefore, we would not talk about book rate of return. We will focus our attention primarily on payback period discounted payback period internal rate of return in addition to NPV. Now, if I may ask you a question. If you observe with this particular figure, the sum of percentages exceeds 100%. Can you think of why? The answer is simple. The sum of percentages in this figure exceeds 100%. Because a manager can make use of more than one technique to evaluate the same project.",
