      "transcript_text": "Let us start with our first technique, which we are already familiar with. It is Net Present Value or NPV. Let us quickly review it. What does NPV depend on? You understand that by now NPV depends on two things. One is, your forecasted cash flows from the project and the second is your opportunity cost of capital for the project. You understand that NPV as a technique uses cash flows. It not just uses some cash flows, but it uses all cash flows from the project and it discounts those cash flows properly. Which means if this is the most important thing, this counts those cash flows depending on their riskiness. And we understand that a project with a positive NPV increases the shareholder wealth and a project with a negative NPV decreases the shareholder wealth. And therefore, we should accept those projects which have a positive NPV and we should reject those projects which have a negative NPV. Now NPV has a very interesting property which is only satisfied by NPV and no other technique that we are going to talk about. So that property which NPV has is called as the adding a property. What is adding a property of NPV? It says that NPV of a combined project is equal to the sum of the NPV's of the individual projects. Now before we move forward, let us recall that the financial goal of the corporation is to maximize the shareholder wealth. And you understand that a project with a positive NPV is going to increase the shareholder wealth. So therefore, positive NPV tells us that the project is going to increase the shareholder wealth and it should be accepted. So in other words, NPV of a project is consistent with the shareholder wealth maximization goal of the firm. Okay. Now how do we define NPV? NPV as you know, it is present value net of initial investment which means that if you subtract your initial outlay from your present value, you will arrive at NPV. Okay. What is our acceptance criterion? We accept those projects which have a positive NPV. Okay. What are the advantages of using NPV as a technique? NPV has several advantages. For example, you know that NPV recognizes the time value of money. NPV also provides a clear benchmark for project acceptance. So for example, when we want to accept a project, what do we look at? We look at whether NPV is greater than zero or not. In other words, zero is our benchmark for project acceptance. And this clearly does not depend on the taste and preferences of managers. Further, since NPV is computed using project cash flows, therefore it is not affected by companies' choice of accounting method. However, NPV has one small disadvantage. And you know what is that? It is that NPV is not as easy to communicate to a layperson as some of the other techniques that we are going to talk about. For example, if you tell a layperson that NPV is 100 rupees, the layperson may not be able to understand what does that 100 mean? Whether it is good enough or not.",
