      "transcript_text": "Having talked about the Payback Period Method, now let us talk about another method which is a modification of the Payback Period Method. This method is called as discounted Payback Period Method. What is discounted Payback Period? As the name implies, it is the number of years it takes you to recover your initial investment from the project in discounted terms. Which means after you take into account time value of money. you accept projects using discounted Payback. So you will accept projects that have discounted Payback Period less than or equal to some specified cut of Period. So essentially, first of all you will compute your discounted Payback Period from the project and then you will compare it with some cut of Period, some specified Cut of Period. And who sets this specified cut of Period? This is set by the management. Now what do you think are advantages of this method? Similar to Payback Period Method, this method is also biased towards liquidity. Which means that this method will also favor those projects that are going to pay back Period Method. And you will now take your value back quickly. This method takes into account time value of money. And what does that translate into? That translates into the fact that discounted Payback Period Method will never accept a negative NPV project. We will talk about in some detail through an example. Before that, let us talk about some disadvantages of the discounted Payback Period Method. Again, similar to simple Payback Period Method, even discounted Payback Period Method does not have a clear benchmark for project acceptance. Which means that your cut of Period is arbitrary. This cut of Period is decided by the management. In other words, your decision for project acceptance or rejection depends on the taste and preferences of managers. Again, similar to our simple Payback Period Method, even this method is biased against long term projects. What does that mean? That essentially implies that discounted Payback Period Method also ignores cash flows after the cut of Period. Let us look at one example to understand discounted Payback Period in some detail. Again, we have three projects. Projects A, B and C. Each of the three projects involve an initial outlay of $2000 today. Project A will give you $500 at the end of year 1, another 500 at the end of year 2 and $5000 at the end of year 3. Project B will give you $500 at the end of year 1 and $1800 at the end of year 2. And finally, Project C will give you $1800 at the end of year 1 and $500 at the end of year 2. Now, you have to compute the discounted Payback Period for each of these projects. Now, to compute the discounted Payback Period for each of the projects, first of all, you have to find out present value of each of the cash flows. Now, whatever investment you are making today, which is minus $2000, the present value of that is minus $2000 itself. Now, what is going to be the present value of $500 that you are going to receive at the end of one year from today from Project A? Now, here you can assume that each of the three projects have an opportunity cost of capital as 10%. So, therefore, what is present value of 500 to P's received at the end of year 1 that will be $500 divided by 1.1. Similarly, what will be present value of $500 received at the end of year 2 that will be 500 divided by 1.1 square. And similarly, what is going to be the present value of $5000 received at the end of year 3 that will be $5000 divided by 1.1 cube. In a similar way, you can compute present values for each of the cash flows from each of the projects A, B and C. And therefore, this is what we have as our modified table where we have present values of each of the cash flows. Now, based on this information, can you compute discounted payback period from Project A? You understand that you recover $455 in discounted terms by the end of year 1 from Project A. By the end of year 2, you recover $455 plus $413 which is $868 in discounted terms by the end of year 2. By the end of year 3, you would have recovered clearly more than $2000 in discounted terms from Project A. So, therefore, what is your discounted payback period from Project A? It is clearly 3 years. Similarly, what is your discounted payback period from Project B? If you see, you recover $455 from Project B by the end of year 1 in discounted terms. By the end of year 2, you recover $455 plus $1480 in discounted terms from Project B which is clearly less than $2000. So, therefore, what is your discounted payback period from Project B? It is clearly not defined. You are never able to recover your initial investment ever from Project B in discounted terms. Similarly, what is your discounted payback period from Project C? As you can see, you can recover your initial investment of $2000 in discounted terms by the end of year 2 from Project C. So, therefore, your discounted payback period from Project C is 2 years. Now, you already know what are going to be NPVs of each of these projects if your opportunity cost of capital is 10%. NPV of Project A will be $26 to $4. Project B will be minus $58. And for Project C, it will be plus $50. So, therefore, the question before you is, if you are cut off period is 2 years. Based on discounted payback period, which projects will you reject and which projects will you accept? You will clearly accept Project C and you will reject projects A and B. Based on the NPV technique, which projects will you accept and which ones will you reject? You will accept projects A and C and reject Project B using the NPV method. Now, the question is, what mistake do you think you are making if you are insisting on only taking projects with a payback period of 2 years or less? Clearly, you are rejecting Project A which you should have accepted. That is the only mistake you are making. Okay? Now, what do you notice from this example? What do you think is the disadvantage of discounted payback period as it comes out from this example? You notice that discounted payback period technique ignores cash flows after the cut off period. For example, in the case of Project A, you would have received a cash flow of $37.57 in discounted terms. However, since this cash flow occurred beyond your cut off period, therefore, it has been ignored. However, discounted payback period method has one important advantage over simple payback period method. And you know what is that advantage? It is that discounted payback period will never accept a negative NPV project. For example, in this case, Project B had a negative NPV. And therefore, this project got rejected by discounted payback period method. But the question is why? Why will we never accept a negative NPV project using discounted payback period method? The answer is simple because if a project has negative NPV, it implies that you will never be able to recover your initial investment from this project in discounted terms ever. And therefore, discounted payback period for a negative NPV project will never be defined. And therefore, discounted payback period method will never accept a negative NPV project.",
