      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about some pitfalls or issues with IRR. Let us look at this project F. This project involves an initial outlay of $1000 and this project is going to give you $3000 at the end of the project. However, this project in year 2 will again require an investment of $2500. Now what is the IRR of this project? If you will try to solve for the IRR of this project, you will not be able to find one. Why? Because there are certain projects, which have negative NPV at all discount rates. You understand that how do we define IRR? IRR is that at discount rate, which makes your NPV equal to 0. Now suppose we have a project of this kind. So we have discount rate on x-axis and we have NPV on y-axis. So if you have a project, if you have a project whose NPV function never cuts the discount rate axis. So in this case, we will not have an IRR. Because IRR is that discount rate, which makes your NPV equal to 0. So this project is similar to project F. Similarly, you could have another project whose NPV is let us say positive at all discount rates. In this case also, the NPV function does not cut the discount rate axis, which means that at all discount rates, the project has a positive NPV. So therefore, it is possible that your IRR does not exist. If your IRR does not exist, what will you compare with opportunity cost of capital to be able to take a decision? You understand that you can take a decision about accepting or rejecting a project by comparing IRR with opportunity cost of capital. You will accept a project if IRR is greater than opportunity cost of capital and you will reject a project if IRR is less than opportunity cost of capital. But for you to be able to take that decision, your IRR has to exist. And as we discussed, it is possible that some projects do not have an IRR. Now let us talk about second issue with IRR. Sometimes your projects may have multiple IRRs. Let us look at project G. For example, project G involves an initial investment of $30 billion and it is expected to produce a cash in flow of $10 billion at the end of each year for the next nine years. At the end of the 10th year, it will incur $1.65 billion of cleanup costs. Now, I must tell you one thing. There can be as many different solutions to a polynomial equation as there are changes of sign. For example, if you try to make NPV function and you try to find out that discount rate, which makes this NPV function, equal to 0, in that case, this particular equation will be an equation of degree 10 and you can have up to 10 routes or solutions. However, when we talk about IRR, we are only interested in positive and real routes. Okay, now in this particular equation, how many changes in sign do you have? If you look at project G, how many changes in sign do you have? You have one change in sign from year 0 to year 1. Where you have change in sign from minus 32 plus 10. You have another change of sign from year 9 to year 10, which is from plus 10 to minus 65. In other words, you have two changes in sign and we have something called as Descartes rule. What does Descartes rule say? As I previously mentioned, Descartes rule says that there can be as many positive and real routes to a polynomial equation as there are changes of sign. Since there are two changes in sign in this equation, we can have two positive and real routes, which means that we can have two IRRs in this particular case. However, remember that it is only a necessary condition but not a sufficient condition to have two routes. Now indeed, in this case, we have two IRRs. Npini of project G is 0 when the discount rate is either 3.5% or it is 19.54%. In other words, we have two IRRs. One is 3.5% and the other one is 19.54%. Now for you to be able to take a decision, what will you do? You will compare it with opportunity cost of capital. You will compare your IRR with opportunity cost of capital. So, you have two IRRs and therefore, which IRR will you compare opportunity cost of capital with? You do not know and therefore, it becomes a problem for us in making a decision. Now let us come to another issue with IRR. Another issue with IRR is that it cannot distinguish between investment and borrowing. As an example, let us look at this table. This table has two projects, project D and project E. Project D involves an initial outlay of $1000 today and it is going to give you a cash inflow of $1500 at the end of year 1. Now cash flows from this project resemble a traditional investment project or a lending kind of a transaction. Typically, what happens in an investment project is that you have an initial outlay, which means a cash outflow today, followed by a series of cash inflow in the future. And the similar thing happens when you lend money to somebody else. Now when you lend money, what happens? Money goes out. Yes and therefore, you have a cash outflow today. However, the person whom you have lent money is going to return the principle along with interest over the term of the loan. And therefore, in the future, you will have cash inflows. So therefore, when you have investment or a lending kind of a transaction, you have cash outflow today, followed by a series of cash inflows into the future. On the other hand, let us look at project E here. In project E here, you will get a cash inflow of $1000 today and you will have a cash outflow of $1500 at the end of year 1. Now, this transaction resembles a borrowing transaction. In this case, you have a cash inflow today because you have borrowed money and therefore, you are receiving it. However, you will have to return this amount along with interest over the term of the loan. So, which means that in the future, you will have cash outflows. Now, if you compute your IRR from project D and project E, what will that come out as? It is simple. If you compute it, your IRR from project D as well as project E will come out as 50%. Now assume that this project has an opportunity cost of 10%. What will your NPV be for each of the projects? It will come out as $364 for project D and minus $364 for project E. So, based on NPV, which project would you accept? Clearly, you would accept project D because it has a positive NPV. Also, you will reject project E because it has a negative NPV. Now, can you make this decision based on IRR? Both the projects remember have the same IRR of 50%. So, in this case, what does the IRR rule tell you? The IRR rule tells you that you should accept a project if your IRR is greater than opportunity cost. However, remember that the IRR rule is correct only for investment or lending kind of transactions. This rule you have to modify in case your transaction is a borrowing kind of transaction. So, in the case of a borrowing kind of a transaction, you accept a project if your IRR is less than opportunity cost of capital. And clearly, in this case, in the case of project E, since your IRR is greater than your opportunity cost of capital, therefore, you will reject this project. You must be wondering, do we have borrowing kind of transactions in our day to day lives? The answer is yes. Look at from the perspective of an insurance company, an insurance company is into sale of insurance policy. So, therefore, what kind of pattern of cash flows will you have for an insurance project? If the company has issued an insurance policy. So, this company is going to receive money today for selling this insurance policy. However, customers are going to make claims in the future, which the company may have to service, which implies that in the future, the company will have cash outflows. So, therefore, what are your pattern of cash flows? So, you have a cash inflow today followed by a series of cash outflows in the future. So, in these kind of projects, you will have to change your IRR rule. You will accept the project based on the IRR rule for a borrowing kind of a transaction if your IRR is less than opportunity cost of capital.",
