      "transcript_text": "Now we will talk about issues that IRR has when we want to rank mutually exclusive projects. You must be wondering what are mutually exclusive projects. So let us talk about different kinds of projects to begin with. We have something called as an independent project. What is an independent project? An independent project is that project whose acceptance or rejection does not prevent the acceptance of other projects under consideration. For example, if there are two projects A and B, we can call them to be independent if we can either accept project A or project B or both. Now let us talk about dependent project. A project whose acceptance depends on the acceptance of one or more other projects is clearly a dependent project. For example, if you can accept project A, only if project B is accepted, then project A is dependent on project B. And we also have something called as mutually exclusive projects. A project whose acceptance prevents the acceptance of one or more alternative projects is what we call as a mutually exclusive project. For example, if you can accept either A, A or B, but not both. In that case, projects A and B are essentially mutually exclusive projects. Now IRR as a Capital Budgeting Technique has one shortcoming. And that shortcoming is that IRR is not a reliable technique when we want to rank mutually exclusive projects. projects. Suppose there are two projects, projects H and I and these are mutually exclusive projects. What does that mean? It means that either you can accept project H or you can accept project I but you cannot accept both the projects. Project H as you can see in the table involves an initial outlay of the project. and it is going to give you $20,000 at the end of one year from today. Project I on the other hand involves an initial investment of $20,000 and it is going to give you $35,000 at the end of one year. Now these projects are mutually exclusive projects which you already have. Now let us say that both the projects have an opportunity cost of capital of 10%. Now what is IRR of project H? If you compute it, IRR is not a very important project. IRR will come out as 100% and for project I, the IRR will come out as 75%. Similarly, if you compute and TV of each of the two projects and TV of project H comes out to be $8,182 and TV of project I comes out to be $11,000. $818,000. Clearly you can see that NTV of project I is higher than the NTV of project H. However, if you look at IRR, IRR of project H is higher than that of project I. So essentially, in this case IRR is telling you that you should accept project H while NTV is telling you that you should accept project I. So what decision will you take? I must tell you that NTV is the most superior of all the techniques. NTV as a number tells you changing shareholder wealth as a consequence of acceptance of the project. And clearly as you know, the financial goal of the firm is to maximize or increase the shareholder wealth. And therefore, we should accept that project which is going to have a higher increase in shareholder wealth, which means that ideally we should accept project I over project H. However, IRR is giving us a different answer. Why? The answer is simple because IRR is not a reliable technique when we want to rank, mutually exclusive projects that differ in their scale of investment. Now, can you somehow use IRR to compare projects that differ in their scale of investment? The answer is yes, but how? So for that, what you have to do is first of all you have to compute incremental cash flows. And then you have to compute IRR on the incremental cash flows. So in this case, we have projects H and I and we will compute cash flows from the incremental project I minus H. What does that represent? That represents incremental cash flows from project I over and above the cash flows from project H. And if you compute that you will have minus $10,000 today followed by $15,000 at the end of one year. And if you compute IRR of this incremental project I minus H, it will come out as 50%. And also if you look at the NPV, the difference in NPV comes out as $3636. Now, which project would you accept based on your IRR of the incremental cash flows? So essentially to be able to take that decision you have to compare IRR of your incremental project with your opportunity cost of capital. And since your IRR is 50% of this incremental project and your opportunity cost of capital is 10% and since 50% is greater than 10% what does that tell you? It tells you that project I is incrementally superior to project H. When the opportunity cost of capital is 10% and therefore you would accept project I over project H. Now the question is when we try to compute incremental cash flows? We could have done either I minus H or we could have done H minus I. So the question before us is how should we compute our incremental cash flows? We should compute our incremental cash flows in such a manner that our first cash flow is negative. In other words, our cash flows from the incremental project should resemble a traditional investment project which has a cash outflow today followed by a series of cash inflows into the future. So since in this case if you do I minus H you will get minus 10,000 dollars today and plus 15,000 dollars at the end of one year from today. So therefore you will have to do I minus H and not H minus I.",
