      "transcript_text": "Now let us try to solve a few problems. Let us look at the first problem. Consider the following projects. So you have three projects, projects A, B and C. Project A involves an initial investment of $1000. Project B, $2000 and Project C, $3000. And each of the projects are going to give you some cash flows in the future. Let us look at the first part. If the opportunity cost of capital is 10%, which projects have a positive and PV. So you can compute NPVs for each of the three projects and by now you know how to compute NPV. So if you compute NPV, it will come out as roughly negative $91 for Project A, $4045 for Project B and $39 for Project C. Okay, so therefore based on NPV, which projects would you accept? You would accept those projects which have a positive and PV. And in this case, clearly projects B and C have a positive and PV and therefore you should accept projects B and C. Let us look at part B. It says calculate the payback period for each project. Okay, how many years does it take you to recover your initial investment of $1000 from Project A? Clearly it takes you one year. And therefore your payback period from Project A is one year. Project B, it takes you two years to recover your initial investment of $2000 from Project B and therefore your payback period from Project B is two years. And similarly, how many years does it take you to recover your initial investment of $3000 from Project C? Clearly as you can see, you recover your investment of $3000 from Project C in four years. So therefore your payback period for Project C is four years. Now let us look at the next part. It says which projects would affirm using the payback rule except if the cut-off period is three years. Which projects will you accept? You will accept all those projects which pay you back in less than three years. And here clearly projects A and B pay you back in less than three years and therefore you will accept projects A and B. Project C on the other hand will pay you back in four years and therefore you will reject Project C. Now let us look at the next part. It says calculate the discounted payback period for each project. Now to be able to compute the discounted payback period, what you have to do is you have to first of all find out present value of each of the cash flows. So for example, if we look at Project A, the present value of $1000 that you are going to spend today is same as $1000. Now from Project A you are going to get $1000 at the end of one year from today and the present value of these $1000 is going to be $1000 divided by 1.10 and which will give you $909. In a similar way, can you not compute the present value of each of the cash flows from each of the projects? Yes you can and therefore once you do that you will have something like this. Now let us compute discounted payback period for each of the projects. What is discounted payback period for Project A? You are never able to recover your initial investment of $1000 in discounted terms ever from Project A and therefore discounted payback period for Project A is not defined. What about Project B? In the case of Project B you recover $909 by the end of year 1 in discounted terms. You recover $909 plus $8 to $6 in discounted terms by the end of year 2 which is still less than $2000 and if you notice you are able to recover your initial investment of $2000 fully in discounted terms by the end of year 3. So therefore your discounted payback period for Project B is 3 years. Now let us look at Project C. How long does it take you to recover your initial investment of $3000 from Project C? It takes you 5 years to recover your initial investment of $3000 from Project C in discounted terms. So therefore your discounted payback period for Project C is 5 years. Now let us look at the last part of this question. It says which projects would have formed using the discounted payback rule except if the cut-off period is 3 years. So which projects would you accept? Clearly you will only accept Project B because discounted payback period of Project B is 3 years which is same as your cut-off period. Here as you can notice you will never be able to accept Project A irrespective of your cut-off period because you are never able to recover your initial investment ever from Project A. In other words if you recall discounted payback period technique will never accept a negative and PV project. Thank you.",
