      "transcript_text": "Now let us look at problem 2. Mr. Simply Simple, the President of Simple Enterprises has to make a choice between two possible investments. So there are two projects, Projects A and B. If you see, Project A involves an initial investment of $400 K today and Project B involves an initial investment of $200 K today. All the figures are in thousands of dollars. Now the problem says the opportunity cost of capital is 9%. Mr. Simple, is tempted to take Project B which has a higher IRR. If you look at the IRR, Project A has an IRR which is given to you as 23% and Project B has an IRR of 36%. And clearly Project B has higher IRR than Project A. Now the first part says, Explain to Mr. Simple why this is not the correct procedure. Why Mr. Simple should not go with the project which has a higher IRR necessarily. The answer is we understand that we have two projects and these two projects are mutually exclusive projects because Mr. Simple, has to make a choice between these two projects. Now these two projects differ in their scale of investment. If we look at Project A, it has a higher scale of investment than Project B. So therefore, even if Project A has lower IRR relative to Project B, it is still possible for Project A to have a higher NPV relative to Project B. So making a choice simply based on higher IRR may not be the correct procedure. Let us look at Part B. It says, show him how to adapt the IRR rule to choose the best project. How can we make use of IRR technique to choose the best project? So here clearly as you can recall, we have to make use of incremental cash flow trick. We have to compute first of all incremental cash flows. And then we have to compute IRR on those incremental cash flows. So let us first of all try to find incremental cash flows. We have Project A and we have Project B. Project A involves an initial investment of $400,000 at the end of one year. Project A will give us $250,000 and another $300 at the end of year two. Project B on the other hand will involve an initial investment of $200K. It will give us $140K at the end of year one and another $179K at the end of year two. Now in this case, shall we do A minus B or B minus A? What do you think? So here, we will do A minus B. Why? Because we want our first cash flow to be negative and it will be negative only if we do A minus B. And if we do so, what does that give us? That will give us an incremental cash flow of minus 200K today, followed by 110K at the end of one year and 121K at the end of the second year. Now, what is NPV of this project A minus B going to be? If we were to construct an NPV function, NPV of this project A minus B, this will be equal to minus 200 plus 110 divided by 1 plus R plus 121 divided by 1 plus R whole square. Now, how do we define IRR? You understand that IRR is that discount rate which makes NPV equal to 0, which means that if we put our NPV equal to 0, in that case, our R will become IRR. So, this implies minus 200 into 1 plus IRR whole square plus 110 into 1 plus IRR plus 121 equals 0. Now let us put 1 plus IRR is equal to x and if we do so, what will happen to our equation? Our equation will become minus 200X square plus 110X plus 121 equals to 0. Now this is a quadratic equation. This is an equation of the form AX square plus BX plus C equals to 0. And here, the solution to this equation is given by minus B plus minus square root of B square minus 4 AC divided by 2A. Here, the term B square minus 4 AC is called as the discriminant. And as long as this discriminant is greater than or equal to 0, this will imply that x will be real. Let us try to solve for x in our equation. So, this implies x equals minus B which is 110 plus minus B square which is 110 square minus 4 into A which is minus 200 into C which is 121. This whole divided by 2 into A which is minus 200. So, this implies that our x equals minus 110 plus minus if you solve for the square root, you will get 330 and this divided by minus 400. Now here, x1, x1 equals minus 110 plus 300 divided by minus 400 and x2 equals minus 110 minus 330 divided by minus 400. So, if you solve for these, what will you get? Here, you will get minus 0.55 as your x1. If you solve for this, you will get your x2 as plus 1.1. Now, remember that when we try to find out IRRs, only positive and real routes are of interest to us. The negative routes are not of interest to us and therefore, the value of x which is of interest to us is 1.1. Now from here, we know that our x is equal to 1 plus IRR. So, therefore, 1 plus IRR equals 1.1. This implies our IRR equals 0.1 or this implies that our IRR on the incremental project A minus B, it comes out as 10 percent. Now, based on this, what decision should we take? We will compare the IRR of the incremental project with the opportunity cost of capital. What is our opportunity cost of capital here? It is 9 percent for both the projects. And since our IRR on the incremental project is higher than the opportunity cost of capital. So, therefore, project A is incrementally superior to project B and therefore, we should choose project A over project B. Now, part C says, show him that the project also has the higher NPV. So, which means that in this case, you have to compute NPV of project A and that of project B and compare the two. So, let us compute NPV of project A. What will that be? That will be minus 400 plus 250 divided by 1.09 plus 300 divided by 1.09. And that will give you an NPV of 81.86K dollars. If you look at NPV of project B, what will that be? That will be minus 200 plus 140 divided by 1.09 plus 179 divided by 1.09 square. What does that give you? That gives you dollar 79.10K. Clearly, you can see project A has higher NPV compared to project B. So, therefore, based on the NPV as well, you will choose project A over project B.",
