      "transcript_text": "Let us now try to compute IRR for this project using Excel. In Excel, to compute IRR, we have a function called IRR. And the only argument that this function requires is the array of cash flows. So, first of all, let us put those cash flows in our Excel. So, we have here IRR 0, IRR 1 and IRR 2. And what are our cash flows? Our cash flow today is minus $4000 cash flow at the end of the first year. is $2000 and cash flow at the end of the second year is $4000. Now, as I mentioned previously, in Excel, to compute IRR, you have a function called as IRR. And you have to supply to this function your array of cash flows. And if you do so, Excel throws up a number. And what is that number? If you see here, your IRR has been readily computed by Excel, which comes out as 28.08%. So, that is how you can make use of Excel to compute IRR. Now, however, here, there is one catch. And the catch is that the Excel also allows you to provide a guess. And by default, that guess is 10%. So, sometimes you have multiple IRRs. And in order to find out multiple IRRs, you have to keep on wearing your guess. And Excel will throw that answer, which is closer to your guess. So, therefore, you can use Excel even to find out IRRs for those projects, which have multiple IRRs.",
