      "transcript_text": "You know how to compute net cash flow from a project. It is nothing but operating cash flow. Plus cash flow from changes in capital expenditure and disposal. Plus cash flow from changes in working capital. You must be wondering, what is working capital? Working capital as the name implies, it is the capital needed for day to day operations of the project or a firm. Since we are talking about project related cash flows, so here therefore it is the capital needed for day to day requirements of the project. Now working capital has a cost of cash flow. 3 main components. The first one is inventory. The second one is accounts receivables or AR in short and the third one is accounts pables or AP in short. So let us describe first of all, the simple cycle of operations of a firm. First of all, what happens is that the firm purchases raw materials. from its suppliers. A firm purchases raw materials from its suppliers and I have denoted raw material by RM. Now if these raw materials have been purchased on credit, it means that the firm is not immediately paying its suppliers for the purchase of raw materials. The firm is not immediately paying its suppliers for the purchase of raw materials. The firm is going to avail some credit period from its suppliers and therefore the firm will pay some time after the purchase of raw materials. So let us say that this is the point in time when the firm is making payment to its suppliers for purchase of raw materials. These raw materials will be converted into finished goods and these finished goods will be sold to the customers of the company. So let us say here, this is the point in time when the company is selling finished goods to its customers. I am denoting finished goods by Fg. Now if the company is selling finished goods on credit, it essentially implies that the customers of the company need not pay the company immediately at the time of the sale. So customers will be paying the company some time after the sale of finished goods to them. So let us say this is the point in time when the company collects cash on sale of finished goods. Now if you look at this particular figure, can you let me know when does the cash go out of business? Obviously if you look here, this is the point in time. When the cash goes out of business. Similarly, can you let me know the point in time when the cash comes back into the business? If you look at this figure, obviously this is the point in time when the cash comes back into the business. And this particular time starting from when the cash goes out, till the time it comes back into the business is what we call as our cash cycle. This also represents the amount of cash which is tied up in the operations of the firm. And how do we find that out? So networking capital is a measure of the amount of cash which is tied up in the operations of the firm. Or in other words, this is the cash which the day to day operations of the firm will. The firm will require. And how do we compute networking capital? Networking capital broadly speaking, it is nothing but current assets minus current liabilities. Your accounts receivables and inventory are principal current assets and your account's payable is the amount of cash. Is essentially your principal current liability. So therefore, your networking capital is accounts receivables plus inventory minus accounts tables. Now, as you can make out, most projects require an initial investment in working capital. And therefore, is it a cash inflow or is it a cash outflow? Clearly it is a cash outflow. You also understand that working capital requirements may change during the life of the project. And in crease in sales, you surely requires an additional investment in working capital. And therefore, what is this additional investment in working capital? Cash inflow or cash outflow for the business. This is clearly cash outflow. Also, you understand that working capital is recovered at the end of the project. Whatever inventories you have left over, these inventories can be sold off. Similarly, whatever sales you have made to your customers on credit, you will be able to collect most of them if not all. So therefore, working capital is recovered at the end of the project. So when the project comes to an end, inventories are sold and AR is collected as I just mentioned. And therefore, recovery of working capital is what is it a cash inflow or a cash outflow? This is clearly a cash inflow. Now let us look at one example. The following table tracks, the main components of working capital over the life of a four year project in rupees. The project is starting in 2026 and the project is going to come to an end in 2030. And we are given with accounts receivables in Ventory and accounts pables at the end of each of the years starting from 2026 until 2030. So what does the question say? The question says, calculate networking capital and cash inflows and outflows due to investment in working capital. Now, you understand that what is networking capital? It is nothing but accounts receivables plus inventory minus accounts tables. So let us compute networking capital for year 2026. For 2026, let us do all the calculations. What is your networking capital? Your networking capital is your accounts receivables plus inventory minus cash. accounts pables. What is your accounts receivables for 2026? It is zero. What is your inventory for 2026? It is 75,000 rupees. What is your accounts payable for 2026? It is 25,000 rupees. So therefore, what is your networking capital for 2026? It is simply 50,000 rupees. The next thing you have to do is you have to compute change in networking capital from one year to another year. So here we are interested in knowing change in networking capital in 2026. So change in networking capital is equal to networking capital in 2026. Minus networking capital in 2025. So what is your networking capital in 2026? It is 50,000 rupees. What was your networking capital in 2025? It is clearly zero because all the three components are essentially zero. So your change in networking capital for the year 2026 is simply rupees 50,000. Now we have to compute cash flow from changes in networking capital. This is simply equal to negative of change in networking capital. Why cash flow from change is in networking capital is simply equal to negative of change in networking capital? The answer is simple. If your networking capital goes up, it is essentially going to be a cash outflow. And therefore, it will have a negative sign. Similarly, if your networking capital comes down, it is essentially going to be a cash inflow and therefore, it will be a positive sign. In a similar way, you can compute all the entries and compute cash flow from changes in networking capital in each of the years starting from 2026 until 2030.",
