      "transcript_text": "Now let us try to solve some problems. You have to identify whether the following activities result in incremental cash inflow, incremental cash outflow or no cash flow at all. The first one. The next one is disposal of plant and machinery with salvage value. So you understand that when the project comes to an end, you dispose of your equipment and therefore you are going to realize some value. and therefore what is it? It is clearly an incremental cash inflow. Let us look at the next one. Initial investment in working capital. Your project requires some initial investment in working capital. What is that? It is clearly an incremental cash outflow. Okay? Working capital from one year to another year. What is that? Is it an incremental cash inflow or is it an incremental cash outflow or is there no cash flow at all? So here your working capital is increasing. Which means that you have to invest some additional amount which is equal to increase in the working capital and therefore this increase in working capital is a cash outflow. The next one. No change in working capital from one year to another year. Clearly it is not an incremental cash flow. Decrease in working capital from one year to another year. So your working capital has come down. So which means that some of the working capital has been released. It means that it is essentially an incremental cash inflow. Next one. Recovery of working capital at the end of the project. What is that? It is also an incremental cash inflow. Because once the project comes to an end, you will be able to recover your investment in inventories and receivables. And clearly this is an incremental cash inflow. Next one. Opportunity cost of using an existing resource. What is this? This is clearly an incremental cash outflow. Why? Because this particular existing resource could have been put to some use. which we cannot do anymore because we have used this in our project. And therefore opportunity cost of using an existing resource is essentially an incremental cash outflow. Some cost. What is some cost? Some cost as we discussed a while ago, it is irrecoverable. And therefore it is not an incremental cash flow. Positive incidental effect. So if a project has a positive effect on the existing business of the firm, this is clearly going to be an incremental cash inflow. Negative incidental effect. It is in a similar way going to have a negative effect on the existing business of the firm. And therefore it is an incremental cash outflow. Next one. A location of existing overhead costs. Clearly your project. If it is not resulting in any additional overhead costs, then it is not a cash flow. In this case, since we are merely allocating existing overhead costs to our project, clearly this is not an incremental cash flow. The next one. Savings in firms taxes due to losses on the project. Since your project is increasing losses. So therefore what you can do. You can use these losses on the project to reduce your profits elsewhere in the firm. And therefore as a consequence of losses on this project, your total taxable income comes down. And therefore savings in firm taxes due to losses on the project is essentially an incremental cash inflow. Next one. Proceeds from borrowing for financing capital expenditure. How should we treat this? We should essentially ignore this. Why? Because this is a financing decision. And we know that the standard capital budgeting practice calls for ignoring financing decisions and treating them separately. Similarly, periodic interest payment on loan taken for financing capital expenditure. What is that? Again, that is a financing decision. And therefore you should ignore it.",
