      "transcript_text": "Let us look at another problem now. You have to analyse a project proposal that requires an investment of rupees 12,000 today in plant and machinery. This machinery can be dismantled and sold for net proceeds estimated at rupees 1,949 rupees at the end of year 7. This is your forecasted salvage value. For tax purposes, the company is required to fully depreciate the capital investment with zero salvage value. Over its useful life of 6 years, using the straight line method of depreciation. If the equipment is sold, you must pay tax at 21% on the difference between the sale price and the net book value of the asset. Refer to the year and forecasts of working capital, sales, cost of goods sold and other project related costs in the table. All the entries in this table are in nominal amounts. Further, the nominal opportunity cost of capital for projects of similar risk has been estimated as 20%. Assume that the company can use the tax laws on the project to shield income from the rest of its business. Assume that all cash flows occur at the end of their respective years. So, the question before you is very, very simple. Is this investment worth undertaking? So, clearly as you can see, this is a big problem. You have to decide whether it is worth undertaking this project. And now, therefore, what will you do? You will therefore compute NPV of this project. And as you know, computation of NPV requires two things. One is your forecasted cash flows from the project and the second one is your opportunity cost of capital. In this case, your opportunity cost of capital is given to you as 20%. So, therefore, your main job in this problem is to estimate project related cash flows. So, as you know, we are given with the estimate of sales, cost of goods sold and other costs excluding depreciation. And these are given to us for next six years. Now, we have all the expenses except depreciation. And therefore, the first thing we are going to do is we are going to compute depreciation in each of the years of the projects useful life. Now, how much capital investment are you making today? You are making an investment of 12,000 rupees today. What is the salvage value? The salvage value is given to you as 1,949. Now, the question is, generally, how do you compute your depreciation expense under the straight line method? Under the straight line method of depreciation, you assume that you will have the same depreciation expense each year over the useful life of the asset. So, therefore, to compute depreciation expense, what you do is you subtract salvage value from the cost and you divide it by its useful life. So, therefore, our annual depreciation expense under the straight line method is going to be our investment cost minus salvage value. Divided by useful life. Now, as we discussed, this project requires an investment of 12,000 rupees today in plant and machinery. So, therefore, the cost is simply 12,000 rupees. It further says for tax purposes, the company is required to fully depreciate the capital investment with zero salvage value over its useful life of 6 years using the straight line method of depreciation. In other words, we are given that the salvage value is zero and our useful life is 6 years. So, salvage value is zero and useful life is 6 years. So, therefore, our annual depreciation expense is going to be 2,000 rupees every year. Now, based on this information, can we not compute profit before tax in each of the years? We can. So, therefore, what is profit before tax going to be? So, our profit before tax is essentially equal to sales minus cost of goods sold or cost in short minus other costs which do not include depreciation minus depreciation. So, let us say that if you have to compute profit before tax in year 1. So, you will look at sales of year 1. What are those? Those are 523 rupees. What are costs of goods sold for year 1? These are 837. What is your estimate of other costs for year 1? It is 2,200 rupees and what is your depreciation expense each year? It is 2,000 rupees. And if you compute this, it will come out to be minus 451 rupees. Now, tax rate, do we have any information on tax rate given to us? Yes, the problem specified that the tax rate is 21 percent and therefore, can we compute taxes for year 1? So, therefore, your tax for year 1, this will be equal to profit before tax for year 1 multiplied by your tax rate. Your profit before tax for year 1 is minus 4 514 into tax rate which is 21 percent. So, therefore, your tax if you will compute it, it comes out to be negative 948 rupees. Now, what is the meaning of negative tax? Does it mean that instead of company paying taxes to the government, the government is paying taxes? The answer is no. It essentially means that because of losses on this project, the company's total profitable income has come down and therefore, the company is able to save 948 rupees in taxes in year 1. Yes. So, now can we not compute profit after tax? Yes. Let us try to compute profit after tax. So, profit after tax for year 1 that will be equal to profit before tax for year 1 minus taxes in year 1. Your profit before tax for year 1 is negative 4 514 and your tax is negative 948. Therefore, what is your profit after tax for year 1? It comes out to be rupees 3,566. In a similar way, you can compute profit before tax taxes and profit after tax for other years as well. Now our next step is to compute operating cash flow or OCF and we will use the bottom up approach. However, you can use any of the approaches. You can also use the top down approach or the tax shield approach and you will get the same OCF irrespective of the approach you use. Now your OCF your operating cash flow for year 1 that is simply equal to your profit after tax for year 1 plus depreciation for year 1. Your profit after tax in year 1 is negative 3 5 6 and your depreciation is plus 2000. So, therefore, your OCF for year 1 will be negative 1 5 6 6 rupees. In a similar way, can you not compute your OCF for all other years? Yes, you can do that and I am leaving that for you to solve it yourself.",
