      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about security risk and return. In general, you can express the risk of a security by expressing all the states and return on that security in each of the states as well as the probability of each of those states. So let us say that we have three states of nature. We have a boom state, we have a normal state and we have a recession state. Now boom state has a 25% chance of occurrence. Normal state has 50% chance of occurrence and recession state has 25% chance of occurrence. Now these states are mutually exclusive and exhaustive. When I say these states are mutually exclusive, it essentially implies that only one state can occur at a particular point in time. When I say that these states are exhaustive, it means that one of the states is not very much of these states has to occur. Now it is given to us that in case it is boom state, you will get 28% return on security A and 10% return on security B. In the normal state, you will get 15% return on security A and 13% return on security B. And finally, in the recession state, you will get negative 2% return on security A and 10% return on security B. Now based on this information, can you compute expected return on security A? So expected return, in general, it is equal to summation P I R I. What is P I here? P I represents the probability of occurrence of state I and what is R I? R I is return in state I. Now let us compute expected return on security A. That is going to be probability of boom state which is 0.25 into return on security A in the boom state which is 28%. Plus probability of normal state which is 0.5 into return on security A in the normal state which is 15% plus probability of state of recession which is 0.25 into return in recession on security A which is negative 2%. And therefore, what will you get as your expected return on security A? You will get 14.0% as your expected return on security A. In a similar way, you can find out expected return on security B. This is equal to 0.25 into 10% plus 0.5 into 13% plus 0.25 into 10% which will give you 11.5% Now, we have to compute standard deviation of return on each of these securities. So, what is going to be standard deviation of return in general? In general, it will be equal to summation P i into R i minus R bar whole square. This whole, you will have to take a square root of. Now, what is P i here? As we discussed previously, P i is the probability of occurrence of state i. R i is the return in state i and what is R bar? R bar here is your mean return or expected return. So, your R bar is expected or mean return. So, let us try to find out standard deviation of returns on security A. What will that be? That will be 0.25 into 28 minus 14 square plus 0.5 into 15 minus 14 square plus 0.25 into minus 2 minus 14 square. And then you will have to take a square root of this whole term. So, this will give us 10.65% in a similar way. Let us compute standard deviation of returns on security B. What will that be? That will be 0.25 into 10 minus 11.5 whole square. So, this way you can find out standard deviation of returns on a security. When you are given with different states, probability of each of those states occurring and the return on the security in each of those and the return on the security in each of the states.",
