          "model_answer": "Specific risk is risk peculiar to a firm or industry, such as a pilot strike affecting the aviation industry. This type of risk can be eliminated by diversification because adding more securities from other industries reduces the impact of firm-specific events. Market risk is risk shared by most businesses, such as a global financial crisis or COVID-19 pandemic, which stems from market-wide fluctuations. This risk cannot be eliminated by diversification because it affects nearly all firms simultaneously regardless of industry. In a reasonably well-diversified portfolio, specific risk is diversified away, leaving primarily market risk as the dominant risk component. Therefore, total risk of a security comprises both specific risk and market risk, but in a diversified portfolio, only market risk persists.",
