      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about a very important model which is called as Capital Asset Pricing Model or CAPM in short. The securities expected return and the return on treasury bills is known as securities risk premium. We also understand that for a well diversified portfolio, the appropriate measure of risk is beta. We also understand that treasury bills which are essentially a risk-free security, they have a beta of zero. And therefore, what is the risk premium? It is also zero. Further, we know that the market portfolio with expected return, RM has a beta of one and therefore, what is the risk premium on the market? It is simply RM-RF. So, we understand that in general, security risk premium is equal to RI-RF. Where I represent RIF is the risk premium is the risk premium on a risk-free asset. Such as a treasury bill, it will be here, RIF is the risk premium on a risk-free asset. Here, I will replace I with F and therefore, my risk premium on a risk-free asset will be RIF-RF which will be zero. What is my risk premium? On the market, here I will denote market by letter M and therefore, I will replace I with M as my subscript and therefore, risk premium on the market will be RM-RF. Now, we also understand that what is beta of the market? If you recall from our discussion, beta of the market is one and what is risk premium on the market which we have just computed? It is RM-RF. Now, let us similarly talk about a risk-free asset. In the case of a risk-free asset, what is beta? The beta is zero and what is risk premium? Risk premium is also zero. So, we know what is the risk premium when beta is zero and when beta is one. But we do not know what is the expected risk premium when the beta is neither zero nor one. In that case, we have something called as capital asset pricing model which was given by William Sharp, John Linton and Jack Trener. They helped answered this question. So, they simply said that the risk premium on a security is proportional to its beta. So, in other words, capital X says that the risk premium on a security i which is obviously, R i minus R f. So, essentially, sharp lintner and Trener, they mentioned that the risk premium on a security varies in direct proportion to its beta. In other words, they mentioned that the risk premium on a security is equal to beta i into R m minus R f. We can rearrange this equation and we can write that our expected return on a security i equals R f plus beta i into R m minus R f. So, we will be using this equation to compute the expected return or required return on a security. So, therefore, as you can see, we can use cap M to find out the opportunity cost of capital for a project. So, essentially, cap M tells you that the opportunity cost of capital for a project depends on the beta of the project. The higher the beta of the project, the higher is going to be the return that you are going to require from the project. Now, let us look at security market line. Now, what is security market line? Security market line is essentially a plot between expected return. And beta. Let me denote beta by this letter and expected return by R. You understand that for a risk-free security, what is the beta? Beta is 0 and what is the return that you expect to be. Let us get from a risk-free security that return is essentially R f. This is your risk-free rate. We understand that for our market portfolio, let me denote it by R m. What is the beta of the market portfolio? The beta of the market portfolio, as you know, it is equal to 1. And what is the expected return on the market? The expected return on the market is R m. So, therefore, this point represents our market portfolio and this point represents our risk-free asset. Now, what is security market line? Security market line, which we refer to as SML in short. So, what is SML? SML is security market line. This essentially is the equation of the capital asset pricing model. Now, if you draw a line which passes through these two points, this line is essentially your security market line. Now, if you remember, the equation of a line is of the form y equals mx plus c. Where what is m? m is the slope of the line and what is c? c is the intercept on the y axis. Here, what is our variable on the y axis? It is R. So, R equals, now what is our slope of this line here? So, if you see our slope is y2 minus y1 divided by x2 minus x1. What is the slope? Alternatively, you can also say that your slope is nothing but tangent. And what is tan theta? It is your perpendicular by base. What is your perpendicular here? It is Rm minus Rf. And what is your base here? It is 1 minus 0. So, here, it is Rm minus Rf divided by 1 minus 0. So, therefore, your slope is Rm minus Rf. So, therefore, the equation of the line is R equals Rm minus Rf. What is our x variable? It is beta. What is our intercept on the y axis? Our intercept on the y axis of this line is Rf. So, this implies that our R equals Rf plus beta times Rm minus Rf. So, as you can see, this is nothing but capital asset pricing model. So, security market line represents your capital asset pricing model.",
