      "transcript_text": "Now the question is how do we measure the cost of equity and the cost of debt? Let us first of all talk about measuring the cost of equity RE. So to measure the cost of equity more often than not, manage the cost of equity equity, mutuals and employees, capital asset pricing model. What does capital asset pricing model say, CAPM says that expected return on a security I equals risk rate plus data of security I multiply by the cost of equity. So Rm minus RF is also called as market risk premium. Now here we can substitute this subscript I with E, A and D. In other words, we can say that our cost of equity will be RF. Plus, beta of equity into Rm minus RF. Similarly for our debt, we can say that the cost of debt or the expected return on debt, RD will be equal to RF plus beta of debt. into RM minus RF. And finally, if we talk about our assets. In the case of assets, our asset cost of capital RE or simply the cost of capital, it will be equal to RF plus beta of assets into RM minus RF. However, many your times the debt of our company is not traded and therefore you will not be able to know the beta of debt. An alternative approach to find RD is to add a spread to the risk rate. Now the question is, what is that spread? What does that spread depend on? That depends on the riskiness of the debt. In other words, that depends on the credit rating of the firm. The higher the credit rating of the firm, the less risky the debt of the firm and therefore the lower the yield spread. In other words, we can say that our RD will be equal to RF plus some yield spread, which will depend on the credit rating of the firm. Okay, here, how do we estimate our beta of equity or beta of debt? To estimate the beta of equity, what we do is we simply regress, you simply regress stock returns on market returns. You will obtain a fitted regression line and the slope of that regression line will give you beta of the stock or beta of equity. In a similar way, if your debt is traded, in that case you can regress your bond returns on market returns. And you obtain a fitted regression line. Whatever is the slope of this line, that will give you beta of debt. So let us look at the security market line. What is security market line? Security market line is a plot of expected return on beta. So we have beta on the x axis and expected return on the y axis. So if we look at this plot, you understand that here you have risk free asset whose beta is 0 and whose expected return is RF. Now that is somewhat more risky than the risk free asset. So which means that the beta of the debt will be greater than 0. Okay. So let us say that beta of the debt is beta D and the return you would expect on this debt therefore would be RD. Similarly, equity which is much more risky than your debt, it will have a much higher beta than the beta of debt. Let us call the beta of equity as beta E and based on this SML. We will require a return RE, commensurate with beta of equity and beta of assets which essentially is weighted average of the beta of debt and beta of equity. It will essentially lie in between the two. So commensurate to beta of assets we have asset cost of capital. So as you can see here, our beta of debt is the lowest of all the three. So beta of debt is lesser than the beta of assets and beta of assets in turn is lower than the beta of equity. And since these beta's determine our expected return, our cost of debt or expected return on debt RD is lower than our asset cost of capital RA. And this RA in turn is lesser than RE.",
