      "transcript_text": "Did you hear about the Mulliez family in France? Where they have more than 1,000 members of the family as shareholders. And they have a very unique motto of involving the family members in every business that they control. And they have an amazing system to manage this family's business. From the Indian context bro, we have companies like Tata's and Godrej we have more than a century old. >> Yes. >> Very interesting how how these family businesses are. And there's a several different interesting elements about a family business, right? And that's why we need this very interesting course. Welcome. And I am Sai Chittaranjan Kalubandi. A faculty in the strategy area and entrepreneurship area of IIM Bangalore. >> And hello, I'm Vivek Srinivasan. I used to be a corporate citizen earlier working in large family houses. And now I run a consulting firm which specializes in family business. So, welcome to this family business strategy course. In this course we will understand how emotionally charged families and rational businesses actually work together, interact with each other to ensure the longevity of the family firm. We begin the course by discussing what makes a family business unique. The several interesting dynamics within a family business as well as different perspectives including historical, financial, and social perspectives on family businesses. We will also see how family businesses change over multiple generations, right? We start with somebody siblings, cousins, and how the family businesses progresses, changes that happen from generation to generation. We will also lead that to how succession happens in a family firm. And from your point of view, how ascension happens for all of you into the family firm. And working in a family business is about working with relatives. >> Yeah. >> Typically, you will end up working in a family business along with your parents, your siblings, uncles, aunts, and several other relatives. And inevitably, this does lead to conflict and negotiation. As well as the interesting question of what is the role of women within family businesses, and how is this changing over a period of time? Conflict, like Professor Sai said, is inevitable in the family business. There are several methods by which you can manage those. We'll talk about family constitutions, councils, forums, all these. How do they manage the conflict as they grow in size? We will also talk about the internationalization of family firms, innovation, and stakeholder and societal engagement of family firms. We will also talk about how institutions in which family businesses are embedded, shape the strategic responses of families and family businesses across the world. And lastly, from a very Indian context, we will understand how HUFs work. It's very, very significant in India. So, in all, welcome to this family business strategy course. I'm sure you'll enjoy this course.",
