      "transcript_text": "Let's start this session by understanding what is really a family business. Or when does a business become a family business? Let's see some examples and try to make sense of those. Let's take Zerodha. This was started by two brothers, Nikhil and Nithin. And uh they've grown very well. Now tell me, will that become a family business because the founders are two brothers? Let's take Mamaearth. This time it's a husband and wife. They have started this company, doing very well again. Therefore, because they two are related, does it become a family business? Suppose there are more relatives. Let's take Byju's. Now there are three relatives in that company. There is Byju Raveendran, there is uh Divya Gokulnath, and there is Riju Raveendran. So more relatives, does it qualify to become a family business? Or these three companies are unlisted. Can we say a listed company can never be a family business? Let's take two examples of listed companies. TCS is a listed company. Can it become a family business? Let's take EaseMyTrip. Now EaseMyTrip is today a listed company. It was started by three brothers. Pitti brothers. Does that become a family business or it cannot be because it is listed? So what really defines a business to be called a family business? There are actually three characteristics that will help us understand what makes a business a family business. The first is ownership. In simple terms, ownership is the shareholding that the family has or the owners have in the business. The second is the management. By management, we mean how the operations of the company are run, how is whose vision is is used for the company? Third is the succession or the intent of the family to make it a business that will last generations. These are the characteristics that will help us determine like all the examples we'll go forward, we'll see which of these will qualify under this. >> [music]",
